{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeNrQnuqKfaKVV33tBqqJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"everything else in the namespace is now cut off. It denies the wrong things and fails to deny the right one.\n\n**16. B.** It becomes *less* restricted — nothing isolates it any more, so all traffic is permitted in both directions.\n\nA Pod is isolated for a direction only if some NetworkPolicy both selects it and names that direction in `policyTypes` [source: k8s-docs-network-policies-depth-2026-08-24]. Relabelling a Pod out of the top-level `podSelector` removes it from the policy's subjects entirely — and since policies are the only thing that isolates, an unselected Pod reverts to the non-isolated default: all inbound and all outbound allowed [source: k8s-docs-network-policies-depth-2026-08-24].\n\n*Why A is wrong, and it is the intuitive miss:* the reflex is that losing a permission makes you more restricted. In this model the policy was not only granting — it was also the *only* thing restricting. Lose the selector and you lose both at once, and the net effect is wide open.\n*Why C is wrong:* selectors are evaluated continuously, not captured at creation. This is Chapter 4's mechanism: the join is by label, live *[cross-bearing: see Ch 4 §5 — labels and selectors as the universal join]*.\n*Why D is wrong:* there is no denied-until-claimed state. Unselected is unrestricted.\n\nThis is a consequence nothing else in the chapter reaches, and it is worth a slow read. A relabelling that looks like tightening — pulling a Pod out of a policy — is the opposite.\n\n**17. D.** Rules being evaluated in the wrong order is not a possible cause.\n\nNetwork policies do not conflict; they are additive, and **order of evaluation does not affect the policy result** [source: k8s-docs-network-policies-depth-2026-08-24]. There is no ordering to get wrong.\n\nA, B and C are all real. A: network policies are implemented by the network plugin, and a resource created without a controller that implements it has no effect [source: k8s-docs-network-policies-depth-2026-08-24]. B: traffic to and from the node where a Pod is running is always allowed, unconditionally [source: k8s-docs-network-policies-depth-2026-08-24]. C: the permitted set is the union of what all applicable policies allow [source: k8s-docs-network-policies-depth-2026-08-24], so another policy's grant is enough on its own.\n\nTwo of the three real causes are in §6; one is in §7. D is the firewall model smuggled back in — and it is smuggled in *as an explanation*, which is exactly how a wrong mental model survives a troubleshooting session.\n\n**18. C.** A `type: LoadBalancer` Service with no provider; a Service whose selector matches no Pods; an Ingress with no controller; a NetworkPolicy on a plugin that does not implement NetworkPolicy.\n\nThe first two are Chapter 9's (§3 and §4). The third is Ch 10 §3 [source: k8s-docs-ingress-depth-2026-08-24]; the fourth is Ch 10 §7 [source: k8s-docs-network-policies-depth-2026-08-24].\n\n*Why A is wrong, and it is the most common answer:* undercounting. Readers reliably produce this chapter's two and forget last chapter's, because the two Chapter 9 instances were taught before the rule had a name. The `<pending>` external address is memorable; the empty EndpointSlice is not.\n*Why B is wrong:* a Deployment always produces a ReplicaSet — the controller for it is built into kube-controller-manager and is always running. It is not an instance of a *missing* component.\n*Why D is wrong:* a Pod with no node is Pending, which is a scheduling outcome, not an absent component; and an Ingress with no `ingressClassName` may still be handled via the default class [source: k8s-docs-ingress-controllers-2026-08-24].\n\nIf you produced all four, §8's argument is one you assembled rather than one you were handed, and the question you take forward from it — *what is watching this, and is it installed?* — is a tool rather than a slogan.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter Summary\n\n| Concept | Remember This |\n|---|---|\n| **The exposure ceiling** | One external address per Service. Fine for one; expensive for fifty. No Service type reads HTTP. |\n| **The layer ladder** | Chapter 9 moves packets to an address (layer 4). §2–§5 read requests (layer 7). §6–§7 go back down to addresses and ports (layer 3/4). Which rung you are on determines what you can know. |\n| **North-south / east-west** | Into the cluster / between Pods. §1–§5 is one; §6–§7 is the other. |\n| **Edge router** | The router enforcing the cluster's firewall policy. A cloud gateway or physical hardware. An Ingress controller may configure it. |\n| **Ingress** | HTTP and HTTPS routes from outside to Services within, controlled by rules on the resource. **Nothing else.** |\n| **The four capabilities** | Externally-reachable URLs, load balancing, TLS termination, name-based virtual hosting. |\n| **Simple fanout** | One address, one host, many paths → many Services. The rule reads the **URI**. |\n| **Name-based virtual hosting** | One address, many hosts → many Services. The rule reads the **host**. |\n| **DNS vs virtual hosting** | DNS resolves a name *before* traffic moves. Virtual hosting sorts traffic that has *already arrived*. Opposite sides of the connection. |\n| **`pathType`** | `Exact`, `Prefix`, `ImplementationSpecific`. Every path must have one. `Prefix` matches element by element, not by string prefix. |\n| **Default backend** | An Ingress with no rules must specify `.spec.defaultBackend`. It also catches any request matching no host or path. |\n| **Ingress controller** | **Required.** Creating an Ingress alone has no effect. Ideally all fit the reference spec; in reality they differ. |\n| **IngressClass** | `ingressClassName` names which controller should fulfil an Ingress. Exactly one default class applies when the field is omitted. |\n| **Frozen ≠ deprecated** | GA + stability guarantees + no removal plans **and** no further development. Both halves. |\n| **Gateway API** | Extensible, role-oriented, protocol-aware. Not built in — installed as custom resources. |\n| **The three roles** | Infrastructure provider → GatewayClass. Cluster operator → Gateway. Application developer → HTTPRoute. |\n| **Gateway cardinality** | Exactly one GatewayClass per Gateway. Many Routes per Gateway. |\n| **NetworkPolicy scope** | Layer 3/4, application-centric, applies to connections with a Pod on one or both ends. Not host isolation. |\n| **The three identifiers** | Pods, namespaces, IP blocks. Selectors for the first two; CIDR for the third, because nothing outside the cluster has labels. |\n| **Non-isolated by default** | A Pod is open in both directions until some policy selects it *and* names that direction. |\n| **Additive, no deny** | Policies never conflict; the permitted set is the union. Order of evaluation is irrelevant. Removing access means removing the grant. |\n| **Both ends** | Source's egress *and* destination's ingress. Either one refusing kills the connection. |\n| **Default-deny by construction** | Empty `podSelector`, both `policyTypes`, no rules. Isolation without permission is denial. |\n| **The two exceptions** | A Pod cannot block access to itself. Node-local traffic is always allowed. |\n| **Plugin dependency** | NetworkPolicies are implemented by the network plugin. No supporting plugin, no effect, no signal. |\n| **Out of scope** | No TLS, no Service-name targeting, no logging, no explicit deny, no loopback blocking, and five more. |\n| **The rule** | An object without its component does nothing. Four instances so far. Ask: *what is watching this, and is it installed?* |\n\n---\n\n## The Voyage Ahead\n\nYou have spent two chapters on the network, and you have been treating one thing as a given the entire time: that a Pod which restarts is a Pod that starts over. Chapter 5 said it directly — Pods are cattle, replaced rather than repaired — and Chapters 6 through 10 have quietly depended on it. A Service can point at an interchangeable set of backends precisely because they *are* interchangeable.\n\nChapter 11 is where that assumption runs out.\n\nSome workloads write things down. A database has a disk, and the contents of that disk are not a detail of the Pod. They are the entire point of running it. Chapter 11 works out what happens to data when the Pod that produced it is deleted, and the answer turns out to be a ladder of three different lifetimes, only one of which survives the thing that created it.\n\nIt also closes a loop this book left open on purpose. Chapter 6 introduced StatefulSet and told you it was about stable *identity*, not about writing to disk, and then admitted the explanation was incomplete and would stay that way until storage arrived. Storage arrives in Chapter 11, and the second half of that answer arrives with it: what a per-replica volume claim is, and why it outlives not just the Pod but the rescheduling.\n\nAnd you will meet the last of the four pluggable interfaces. You have collected three now: CRI at the container runtime, CRDs at the API, CNI at the network. Chapter 11 brings CSI, and by the time Chapter 17 collects all four in one place, the shape will be so familiar that the collection will feel like recognition rather than instruction.\n\n<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: \"the four pluggable interfaces\" is this book's own phrase for CRI + CNI + CSI + CRDs, per the term-ownership ledger; Ch 17 §4 owns it and shipped Ch 2 §4 already points at that wording. It is deliberately NOT tagged to a snapshot, because the documentation's own list of extension points is longer — six extension points, of which the infrastructure tier alone names five items (device plugins, storage/CSI, network/CNI, container runtime/CRI, and kubelet image credential providers), with CRDs listed separately under API extensions [k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23]. Ch 17 §4 covers both the book's four and the documentation's wider list; this sentence is the book's coinage and should stay untagged. Count corrected here from \"two\" to \"three\" — the reader met CRDs at Ch 6 §8. -->\n\nOne last thing to carry across the chapter boundary. You will meet several objects in Chapter 11 that describe storage without providing any, and at least one arrangement where a claim sits unbound because the thing that would satisfy it has not been installed.\n\nYou know what question to ask about that now.\n\n> *\"An object is a record of intent. Intent does not act. Something has to be watching.\"*"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":64065,"cache_read_input_tokens":279067,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":64065},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0026bdf2-daef-4108-a4d5-2f79971a6924","uuid":"e1b34dcb-3d99-4f75-81bf-73e71e887f16","timestamp":"2026-08-25T04:42:02.119Z","request_id":"req_011CeNrQjLmZm2NGztg5SPCM"}
